            <section id="introduction">
                <h2>Why Budgeting Matters</h2>
                <p>A budget isn't about restricting your freedom; it's about giving yourself permission to spend without guilt. By tracking your income and expenses, you take control of your financial future, reduce stress, and build wealth over time.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="frameworks">
                <h2>Popular Budgeting Frameworks</h2>
                
                <h3>1. The 50/30/20 Rule</h3>
                <p>Ideal for beginners, this method divides your after-tax income into three distinct categories:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li><strong>50% Needs:</strong> Groceries, housing, basic utilities, insurance, and minimum debt payments.</li>
                    <li><strong>30% Wants:</strong> Dining out, entertainment, travel, and hobbies.</li>
                    <li><strong>20% Savings & Debt:</strong> Extra debt payments, emergency fund, and retirement investments.</li>
                </ul>

                <h3>2. Zero-Based Budgeting</h3>
                <p>Popularized by financial apps like YNAB (You Need A Budget), this method requires you to allocate every single dollar before the month begins. Your Income minus your Expenses (including savings) should equal exactly zero.</p>

                <h3>3. Pay Yourself First (Reverse Budgeting)</h3>
                <p>Focuses entirely on your savings goals. You immediately transfer a set amount into savings and investments as soon as you get paid. The rest can be spent however you like, eliminating the need to track every penny.</p>
            </section>

            <section id="best-practices">
                <h2>6 Core Best Practices</h2>
                
                <ol>
                    <li><strong>Track Your Expenses:</strong> You can't manage what you don't measure. Use an app, a spreadsheet, or a notebook to track where your money actually goes for at least 30 days.</li>
                    <li><strong>Build an Emergency Fund:</strong> Before aggressively paying down low-interest debt or investing, save $1,000 for unexpected expenses. Eventually, grow this to cover 3 to 6 months of living expenses.</li>
                    <li><strong>Automate Everything:</strong> Set up automatic transfers for your savings and automatic payments for your bills. Automation removes the temptation to spend money meant for saving.</li>
                    <li><strong>Plan for Sinking Funds:</strong> Irregular expenses (like car maintenance, holiday gifts, or annual subscriptions) shouldn't be emergencies. Divide the annual cost by 12 and save that amount monthly.</li>
                    <li><strong>Review and Adjust Monthly:</strong> Your budget is a living document. Sit down once a month to review what worked, what didn't, and adjust your categories for the upcoming month.</li>
                    <li><strong>Forgive Yourself:</strong> You will overspend occasionally. Don't abandon the entire budget because of one mistake. Simply adjust your other categories to cover the difference and move on.</li>
                </ol>

                <div class="highlight">
                    <strong>Pro Tip:</strong> Wait 24 to 48 hours before making any non-essential purchase over $50. This "cooling-off" period drastically reduces impulse buying.
                </div>
            </section>
